 \paragraph{Audit budget.} Let $\hat S$ be the expected honest first-best surplus over $T$ rounds and $m = \hat S / n_{\mathrm{FB}}$ the mean surplus per first-best served job. The operator's audit budget is $B = \beta \hat S$ and the audit cost $\alpha = \rho m$, so the allowance is $B/\alpha = (\beta/\rho)\, n_{\mathrm{FB}}$ audits; unspent budget carries over.
 
-\paragraph{Identities.} Each provider acts through a current identity. In any round it may retire the identity and re-enter under a fresh one at entry cost $e \ge 0$ ($e = 0$ in both tests). Rules see identities only as opaque tokens, must be invariant to relabelling them, and cannot link a fresh identity to its provider. An identity's age is the number of rounds since its first offer. Honest providers never replace their identities, so in this model a new identity is always a deviating one, except in the honest-replacement diagnostic of the second test; honest churn is outside the model (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}).
+\paragraph{Identities.} Each provider acts through a current identity. In any round it may retire the identity and re-enter under a fresh one at entry cost $e \ge 0$ ($e = 0$ in both tests). Rules see identities only as opaque tokens, must be invariant to relabelling them, and cannot link a fresh identity to its provider. An identity's age is the number of rounds since its first offer. Every identity is new at the start. The honest opponents never replace their identities, so after the start a new identity belongs to a provider that has replaced, usually the focal one; a truthful focal provider may also replace after a false failure, so a new identity marks a replacement history, not dishonest delivery. Churn of honest identities is outside the model, except for the single replacement of the second test's honest-replacement diagnostic (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}).
 
 \paragraph{Admissible rules.} A rule reads only the current offers and job values, the audit results it bought, its own memory, and the configuration. Payments are funded by buyer escrow. The only consequences are a refund of up to the escrow of an audited job that failed; a ban of the identity for at most 50 rounds; holding back up to three rounds of an identity's receipts as a bond; and allocation priority and audit selection as functions of the allowed inputs. There are no entry fees, no collateral before receipts, no identity certification, and no consequences for unaudited jobs.
 
@@ -73,7 +73,7 @@ We study what checking alone can do when the usual remedies against cheap identi
 
 \paragraph{The factorial (second test).} The eight factorial rules and the comparators share B3's allocation and payment, refund-and-ban enforcement, no bond, the allowance $A = \lfloor B/\alpha \rfloor$, and the fixed pair $\tau = \const{0.16}$, $L = \const{10}$, which the registered tuning of B3 and of R3 had each chosen. They differ in three parts: a priority phase P (served jobs of identities younger than $L$ first, newest first), newest-first residual targeting G ($\lceil r/2 \rceil$ residual audits to the newest identities), and pacing S (R3's terminal reserve and even early quota, against B3's spread schedule). The rules $F_{PGS}$ include $F_{000} = $ B3 and $F_{111} = $ R3. B2 keeps its posted prices and serves only as a reference at the headline market.
 
-\paragraph{Verified-history targeting (second test).} The comparator family adapts credibility-based spot-checking and BOINC's adaptive replication to a fixed audit quota. An identity's credibility $p_i$ is its number of passed audits since its creation or its last failure. It is updated in a batch after each round's audits: if any audited job of the identity failed in that round, $p_i = 0$ for the next round, whatever its passes in the batch; otherwise $p_i$ grows by the round's passes. A replacement starts at zero, and audits selected in the current round are not passes. C-thr($K$) treats an identity as unproven while $p_i < K$ and audits unproven identities first, in ascending $p_i$; it then selects $\lceil r/2 \rceil$ residual jobs by ascending $p_i$ plus the picks already assigned to that identity in the round, and the rest uniformly. C-mem($K$, $M$) counts only the passes of the last $M$ rounds, so scrutiny returns to identities that are not audited. C-taper($K$) and C-boinc have no priority phase: they audit by weighted sampling without replacement, with weight $K/(p_i + K)$ (C-taper) or $1$ if $p_i < 10$ and $1/p_i$ otherwise (C-boinc, after BOINC's trust threshold of 10~\cite{boincwiki}); the weights are relative selection weights, not audit probabilities. Twelve configurations were compared in development at the fixed pair and R3's pacing, by the development tuning objective, the smaller of a gross-efficiency margin and an attacker-gain margin at the headline market and a development market; at every configuration the efficiency margin was the smaller one, so the selection was in effect on gross efficiency, and the configurations' attacker gains were not recorded; the two best were rechecked at full budget (runner-up C-thr($K = \const{12}$)), and the selected comparator is C$^* = $ C-mem($K = \const{6}$, $M = \const{20}$). C$^*$ is the best of four tested adaptations; it is not the strongest possible credibility rule, and R3 embodies earlier development choices that the comparison does not equalise. C$^*$/S0 is C$^*$ with B3's spread pacing.
+\paragraph{Verified-history targeting (second test).} The comparator family adapts credibility-based spot-checking and BOINC's adaptive replication to a fixed audit quota. An identity's credibility $p_i$ is its number of passed audits since its creation or its last failure. It is updated in a batch after each round's audits: if any audited job of the identity failed in that round, $p_i = 0$ for the next round, whatever its passes in the batch; otherwise $p_i$ grows by the round's passes. A replacement starts at zero, and audits selected in the current round are not passes. C-thr($K$) treats an identity as unproven while $p_i < K$ and audits unproven identities first, in ascending $p_i$; it then selects $\lceil r/2 \rceil$ residual jobs by ascending $p_i$ plus the picks already assigned to that identity in the round, and the rest uniformly. C-mem($K$, $M$) counts only the passes of the last $M$ rounds, so scrutiny returns to identities that are not audited. C-taper($K$) and C-boinc have no priority phase: they audit by weighted sampling without replacement, with weight $K/(p_i + K)$ (C-taper) or $1$ if $p_i < 10$ and $1/p_i$ otherwise (C-boinc, after BOINC's trust threshold of 10~\cite{boincwiki}); the weights are relative selection weights, not audit probabilities. Twelve configurations were compared in development at the fixed pair and R3's pacing, by the development tuning objective, the smaller of a gross-efficiency margin and an attacker-gain margin at the headline market and a development market; at every configuration the efficiency margin was the smaller one, so the selection was in effect on gross efficiency; at the inner search budget every configuration's selected attack had zero or negative gain, and in the full-budget rechecks the runner-up left a lower D2+H3 gain than C$^*$ (\const{0.076} against \const{0.097} at the headline market, \const{0.018} against \const{0.038} at dev0), so a comparator selected on attacker gain would likely have been the runner-up; the two best were rechecked at full budget (runner-up C-thr($K = \const{12}$)), and the selected comparator is C$^* = $ C-mem($K = \const{6}$, $M = \const{20}$). C$^*$ is the best of four tested adaptations; it is not the strongest possible credibility rule, and R3 embodies earlier development choices that the comparison does not equalise. C$^*$/S0 is C$^*$ with B3's spread pacing.
 
