Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free:
Two Registered Tests of an Audit Rule
Ömer Demirel
Draft 9, November 2026

Abstract
A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider
can return at once under a new identity. We study a repeated procurement market with noisy
delivery, costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, without entry fees, identity certification, or collateral. For the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with
refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, free replacement makes the ban costless, because
replacement restores eligibility before the next allocation and preserves the payoff-relevant future states, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule
we study, R3, audits every job of a new identity while the budget allows and afterwards keeps
targeting the newest identities. We report two registered tests; the second compares R3 with
a verified-history comparator, C∗ , at matched budget and sanctions on six market instances
drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash. We report four findings. First, pooled over the
six markets, R3 and C∗ are “equivalent, direction R3 lower” within a pre-set margin of ±0.05
(C∗ minus R3 under the D2+H3 attacks: 0.021); treated as a sample of markets, the per-market
D2+H3 differences give an exploratory t interval of [−0.013, 0.054], so this analysis does not
establish the ordering beyond these markets, and the second attack family returned the first
family’s winner in 10 of 12 cells. Second, newest-first targeting does almost all the work: once it
is in place, R3’s priority window changes the attacker’s gain by only −0.001. Third, the targeting also falls on honest newcomers: in a registered diagnostic at the headline market, R3 audits
0.917 of an honest replacement identity’s jobs in its second ten rounds, against 0.161 under C∗ .
Fourth, under the prescribed D2+H3 searches, R3 lowers the attacker’s measured gain relative
to uniform audits on every fresh market, but the registered replication passes on 3 of six; at the
headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks lower the gross efficiency of all three
core rules to about 0.840. All claims concern the prescribed searches and frozen references on
the evaluated markets (the six fresh markets from one neighbourhood for the comparisons, the
headline market for the diagnostic and the welfare attacks), fixed rule parameters, the searched
attacker families, and honest opponents that never replace their identities, so population churn
among honest providers is untested; C∗ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect.

1

Introduction

A buyer of computation pays for work whose quality it cannot see. It can check a few jobs at a cost
and sanction a provider whose check fails. Exact checks of computation now exist, but they are
expensive and run after the response is served. FlashProve, for example, proves that a committed
integer model produced a given token under greedy decoding; the provider generates proofs after the
response is served, at about 70 to 95 times the cost of one forward pass [27, p. 18], for a window of
1024 tokens per request, and the client checks each proof on a CPU [27, pp. 16–17]. Its Lean proofs
cover the protocol; the equivalence of the executable prover and verifier to it is not proved [27, p. 17].
1


